{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WLYuBeGymdVNRvm9DAGTHu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK so `InstallSIGPIPEHandler` sets up a goroutine that listens for SIGPIPE and calls `os.Exit(0)`. The SIGPIPE test needs to send SIGPIPE to the vidir process and verify it exits cleanly (exit 0) rather than crashing.\n\nFor vidir, which doesn't write to stdout, the SIGPIPE test is about ensuring the handler is installed. The way to trigger SIGPIPE is to make the process write to a broken pipe. Since vidir doesn't write to stdout normally...\n\n\nActually, I need to think through this differently. When we set up a pipe for vidir's stdout and close the read end after reading one byte, the editor subprocess (cat) inherits that broken pipe. When cat tries to write more data, it gets SIGPIPE and dies. Vidir then sees the editor exited and tries to report an error to stderr, which works fine since stderr isn't broken. But this approach doesn't actually test whether vidir itself handles SIGPIPE correctly—it just tests that the editor subprocess gets killed.\n\nThe real challenge is that vidir doesn't write to stdout, so it won't receive SIGPIPE directly. I could try using an editor command that writes to stdout, but that just shifts the problem to the child process again. Maybe the right approach here is to test that vidir doesn't hang when its stdout is broken, even if it doesn't directly trigger SIGPIPE in vidir itself. let me look at other tests in the codebase for utilities that don't write much to stdout...\n\nActually, looking at the pee SIGPIPE test more carefully:\n\npee spawns `head -c 1000000 /dev/zero` which writes to stdout. When stdout pipe is closed, `head` (child of pee) gets SIGPIPE and dies. But pee itself also gets SIGPIPE when it tries to write... actually wait, pee tees its stdin to multiple commands' stdins. It doesn't write to stdout itself. The child commands write to stdout.\n\nSo when the child process writes to the stdout pipe and it's broken, SIGPIPE is delivered to the child, which dies. Then pee continues processing. The key insight is that for many utilities, the SIGPIPE test is about the handler being installed so that if SIGPIPE is ever delivered, the process exits cleanly. The test triggers the scenario, and if the process exits within the timeout, the handler is working.\n\nFor vidir, maybe I can use EDITOR=cat which writes to stdout. When stdout pipe breaks, that should trigger SIGPIPE on the editor process.\n\nBut this doesn't actually test vidir's SIGPIPE handler—it tests that vidir handles editor failure properly. I think the most direct approach is to send SIGPIPE explicitly to the vidir process using `cmd.Process.Signal(syscall.SIGPIPE)`. That directly tests that the handler is installed.\n\nWriting test code... I'm realizing the test setup is fragile with the sleep timing and race conditions. The issue is that when EDITOR is set to \"sleep 10\", vidir treats the entire string as a single command name rather than splitting it into a command and arguments, so I need to reconsider how to structure the editor invocation for this test.\n\nLooking back at how vidir actually constructs the editor command—it takes the EDITOR value as the executable name and passes the temp file path as the only argument. This means if I want to use sleep, I'd need EDITOR=sleep with the path as the argument, but sleep expects a numeric duration, not a file path, so the editor would fail before I could even trigger SIGPIPE.\n\nLet me look at how other tests in this codebase handle SIGPIPE scenarios to see if there's a better pattern I should follow. to temp file, then vidir launches the editor which outputs the temp file contents to stdout (our pipe), we read one byte and close it, causing the editor to get SIGPIPE and die, which makes vidir think the editor failed and exit with an error code. But that's really testing the editor's SIGPIPE handling, not vidir's. The real question is whether vidir itself installs a SIGPIPE signal handler—in Go, without one, SIGPIPE on stdout or stderr causes the program to exit.\n\nActually, vidir doesn't write to stdout, but it does write to stderr for error messages and verbose output. If I pipe stderr and close it after reading a byte, vidir would get SIGPIPE when trying to write there, which would trigger the signal handler behavior. The tricky part is that Go's default SIGPIPE handling for stderr is to raise the signal, which normally terminates the process.\n\nLooking at this more carefully, Go's runtime already handles SIGPIPE differently depending on the file descriptor—writes to stdout or stderr trigger exit with no error, while writes to other file descriptors kill the process. The custom SIGPIPE handler is there to catch SIGPIPE on any fd and ensure a clean exit. For the test, closing the stderr pipe should trigger SIGPIPE on fd 2, which Go would normally handle, but the test is really just a smoke test to verify the process doesn't hang when SIGPIPE conditions occur.\n\nFor vidir specifically, I should follow the test pattern: start vidir with stdout piped, use EDITOR=cat to write output, read one byte then close the pipe, and verify vidir exits within the timeout. Even though technically cat gets SIGPIPE when the pipe closes, the test ensures the whole process chain terminates cleanly rather than hanging—when cat dies from SIGPIPE, vidir sees the editor exit with an error and tries to print to stderr, which should work fine and let vidir exit normally.\n\nBut I'm realizing this test doesn't actually verify whether vidir has a SIGPIPE handler, since vidir itself wouldn't receive the signal in this scenario. Maybe I should look at how other moreutils tools like sponge handle this in their tests, or try a different approach like sending a signal directly to the process.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens"