TOR=true will properly replace the default editor.\n\nFor the SIGPIPE test though, I'm manually appending environment variables, which could create duplicates if EDITOR is already set. The exec.Cmd.Env behavior on Unix systems is a bit tricky here—I need to be careful about whether the first or last occurrence takes precedence, since that affects whether my override actually works.\n\nBuilding the environment properly... I'm realizing that when I append environment variables to `os.Environ()`, duplicates will cause issues—Go's `os.Getenv` returns the first match, so if EDITOR was already in the parent environment, my appended value won't override it. I need to filter out existing keys before adding the overrides to ensure the child process gets the right values.\n\nWriting helper function... Writing test file structure... Adding more test cases... Writing test logic... I'm reconsidering whether I actually need the `mergeEnv` helper function—it's only used by the SIGPIPE test, while the DiffTest framework already handles environment merging for other tests. For the vidir SIGPIPE test, I need to set the EDITOR variable, so let me think about whether there's a cleaner approach than creating a separate helper. I'm creating a helper function that properly merges environment variables by filtering out any keys we want to override before appending the new values, which eliminates duplicates and ensures our overrides take effect. Now I'm ready to write the actual implementation.\n\nActually, I should double-check the `editor_noop` test behavior—when vidir gets an unchanged listing from the editor, both the Go and Perl versions should exit silently with code 0 and produce no output, so that should work fine. I'll start writing the test file now, making sure to follow the copyright header pattern from the existing tests.\n\nWriting test file structure... Writing the import block...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_t